Subject: [PATCH 03/12] R80b, part 2, patch 3 (cld-advisor, at main's word in
 M14; Review 80, a reader's finding F5, checked by main with a plant of its
 own that passes): THE FILE THAT SAYS WHICH VERSIONS MADE THE RUN IS HELD, AND
 THE WEAKER COMPARISON READS EVERY VALUE.

WHAT STOOD. Where the libraries are not the ones that made the committed run, the lock compares less: the lines to eight digits, the checks, one table of counts. The file that names those libraries decides which comparison is made, and nothing held it. Main's plant gives the file another version and changes a number of part A in the script: the run goes to the weaker comparison on the machine that made it, and the number is not among what that comparison reads.

WHAT IS CHANGED. The file is held to what it says, in the lock, so that a change is in two places. The weaker comparison reads every value of the data file that is not a check: whole numbers and words exactly, decimals to eight digits.

THE PLANTED CASES, in the patch's own test of the weaker comparison: a whole number of part A changed and a decimal of part B changed in its third digit are refused; the same decimal changed past its eighth digit is let be. Each of the three passes the comparison as it was. The file with another version is not the file held.

ON THIS MACHINE the libraries are not the run's (another version of the language), so the weaker comparison is the one the lock makes here on every run: it passes with every value read.

The lock: 17 tests pass. 0 of 19.
